# CHANGELOG.md is APPEND-ONLY: every PR adds its own `### ...` entry under the shared
# `## Unreleased` anchor, so a conflict here is two PRs both appending, never two PRs
# disagreeing about the same line. Union keeps both sides instead of stopping the merge.
#
# GITHUB DOES NOT HONOUR THIS. It affects LOCAL merges only. Measured 2026-08-08 on this
# repo, after the attribute was on main: 0 of 19 CONFLICTING PRs flipped to MERGEABLE,
# across TWO settled trigger-and-read cycles (GitHub computes mergeability lazily, so one
# unchanged reading is indistinguishable from a stale one and was not trusted alone). The
# real server-side merge agrees with the flag: `gh pr update-branch` refused cp#332 AND
# cp#350, 2 of 2, both of which merge CLEAN locally under this attribute. Positive control,
# same token and session: the same command SUCCEEDED on cp#353 and advanced its branch, so
# the refusals are the server's answer and not a broken tool.
#
# THIS FILE DOES NOT DRAIN THE LEGACY QUEUE. An earlier version of this comment claimed it
# did, and that claim was wrong. Measured 2026-08-08 on cp#336: git reads a file's merge
# attribute from the PRE-MERGE working tree. Draining means checking out the PR branch and
# merging main into it, so the tree git consults is the PR BRANCH, and every legacy PR was
# authored before this file existed (`git cat-file -e cae0bd9e:.gitattributes` -> missing).
# The attribute is therefore absent at the moment it would have to apply, `git check-attr
# merge -- CHANGELOG.md` reports `unspecified`, and the merge conflicts exactly as if this
# file were not here.
#
# The instrument lies about this in two ways, both of which fooled the first pass:
#   - `git check-attr` run AFTER a conflicted merge reports `union`, because the merge
#     itself pulled this file in from main. It is describing a state that did not exist
#     when the driver was chosen. Query it on the clean pre-merge checkout or not at all.
#   - Merging the PR head INTO a main checkout DOES apply union and looks like proof. It is
#     the opposite direction from a drain: there the tree git consults is main, which
#     carries the attribute. Direction is the whole variable.
#
# THE RECIPE THAT ACTUALLY WORKS is repo-local and branch-independent, so it does not care
# what the PR branch predates:
#
#     echo 'CHANGELOG.md merge=union' >> "$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/info/attributes"
#     git merge origin/main
#
# Verified bidirectionally on cp#336, same commits both arms, attribute the only variable:
# with it `rc=0` and zero conflict markers; with it removed `rc=1` and one marker. Use
# --git-common-dir, not --git-dir: inside a linked worktree --git-dir resolves to
# .git/worktrees/<name>, whose info/attributes git does not consult, and the attribute
# silently fails to arm (that failure is what made the first negative control meaningless).
#
# SO WHY IS THIS FILE STILL HERE. One narrow live effect: cp#358's changelog-entry check
# accepts EITHER a CHANGELOG.md touch OR a changelog.d/ fragment during the migration
# window, so a branch cut from main AFTER 2026-08-07 may still edit CHANGELOG.md directly.
# Those branches DO carry this attribute, so union applies to them. That population is the
# only thing it serves.
#
# NOT THE REAL FIX. cp#358's changelog.d/ fragments are. They cover every NEW PR; this
# covers neither the backlog nor anything past the migration window.
#
# REMOVE THIS once the migration window closes and the legacy queue is drained. After that
# nothing should be touching CHANGELOG.md directly, so the attribute is pure hazard: it
# would silently change local merge behaviour for no remaining reason.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
